### What is the org.json approach?

The simplest approach uses the `org.json` library. `XML.toJSONObject(xmlString)` returns a `JSONObject`, and `XML.toString(jsonObject)` converts back. This is fast and needs no Java classes, but it is purely structural.

```java
package com.bank.util;

import org.json.JSONObject;
import org.json.XML;

public class QuickConvert {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String xml = "<payment><id>PAY-001</id><amount>50000</amount>" +
                     "<currency>INR</currency></payment>";

        JSONObject json = XML.toJSONObject(xml);
        System.out.println(json.toString(2));

        String backToXml = XML.toString(json);
        System.out.println(backToXml);
    }
}
```

```text
{
  "payment": {
    "id": "PAY-001",
    "amount": 50000,
    "currency": "INR"
  }
}
<payment><id>PAY-001</id><amount>50000</amount><currency>INR</currency></payment>
```

### What are the limitations of org.json?

org.json is lossy and ambiguous. It cannot distinguish XML attributes from child elements. A single repeated element might become an object or an array depending on whether there is one or many. And since XML has no native number type, round-tripping can turn a number into a string.

```java
// Ambiguity: one <item> becomes an object, two become an array
String xml1 = "<order><item>Loan</item></order>";
String xml2 = "<order><item>Loan</item><item>Card</item></order>";

System.out.println(XML.toJSONObject(xml1));
// {"order":{"item":"Loan"}}           – item is a String

System.out.println(XML.toJSONObject(xml2));
// {"order":{"item":["Loan","Card"]}}  – item is an Array!
```

### What is the Jackson approach?

With Jackson you define a typed Java class, read XML with `XmlMapper`, and write JSON with `ObjectMapper`. Because both formats go through the same object, the conversion is type-safe.

```java
package com.bank.dto;

import com.fasterxml.jackson.dataformat.xml.annotation.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@JacksonXmlRootElement(localName = "payment")
public class Payment {
    @JacksonXmlProperty(isAttribute = true)
    private String id;
    private BigDecimal amount;
    private String currency;
    // getters and setters
}
```

```java
package com.bank.util;

import com.bank.dto.Payment;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.dataformat.xml.XmlMapper;

public class TypedConvert {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        String xml = "<payment id=\"PAY-001\"><amount>50000</amount>" +
                     "<currency>INR</currency></payment>";

        // XML → Java object
        XmlMapper xmlMapper = new XmlMapper();
        Payment payment = xmlMapper.readValue(xml, Payment.class);

        // Java object → JSON
        ObjectMapper jsonMapper = new ObjectMapper();
        String json = jsonMapper.writerWithDefaultPrettyPrinter()
                .writeValueAsString(payment);
        System.out.println(json);

        // JSON → Java object → XML
        Payment fromJson = jsonMapper.readValue(json, Payment.class);
        String backToXml = xmlMapper.writeValueAsString(fromJson);
        System.out.println(backToXml);
    }
}
```

```text
{
  "id" : "PAY-001",
  "amount" : 50000,
  "currency" : "INR"
}
<payment id="PAY-001"><amount>50000</amount><currency>INR</currency></payment>
```

Notice that `id` was an XML attribute (not a child element), and the typed object preserved this distinction through the round trip.

### Why is BigDecimal critical for money fields?

XML has no native number type, so everything is text. Without a typed object, a round trip through XML can turn a JSON number `50000` into a string `"50000"`. With a typed `Payment` class that declares `amount` as `BigDecimal`, the conversion is safe regardless of format.

```java
// Wrong – double loses precision:
private double amount;  // 50000.10 might become 50000.09999999...

// Correct – BigDecimal preserves exact value:
private BigDecimal amount;  // 50000.10 stays exactly 50000.10
```

### How do you handle XML namespaces?

Legacy banking XML often carries namespaces. Jackson handles namespaces with `@JacksonXmlProperty(namespace = "...")`. For strict validation, pair conversion with an XSD validator.

```java
@JacksonXmlRootElement(localName = "payment",
        namespace = "http://schema.bank.com/payments/v1")
public class Payment {
    @JacksonXmlProperty(isAttribute = true)
    private String id;
    private BigDecimal amount;
    private String currency;
}
```

### How do you handle lists in XML?

XML wraps repeated elements differently than JSON arrays. Jackson's `@JacksonXmlElementWrapper` controls whether a list has a wrapper element.

```java
public class Statement {
    @JacksonXmlElementWrapper(localName = "transactions")
    @JacksonXmlProperty(localName = "transaction")
    private List<Transaction> transactions;
}
```

```text
// XML with wrapper:
<statement>
  <transactions>
    <transaction><amount>5000</amount></transaction>
    <transaction><amount>3000</amount></transaction>
  </transactions>
</statement>

// JSON:
{ "transactions": [ { "amount": 5000 }, { "amount": 3000 } ] }
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is using org.json for financial data where type safety matters. The second mistake is using `double` for money instead of `BigDecimal`. The third is forgetting that XML attributes require the `isAttribute = true` annotation in Jackson.

```java
// Wrong – org.json for banking payments (lossy, type-unsafe):
JSONObject json = XML.toJSONObject(partnerXml);
BigDecimal amount = json.getBigDecimal("amount"); // may fail if string

// Correct – Jackson typed conversion:
Payment payment = xmlMapper.readValue(partnerXml, Payment.class);
BigDecimal amount = payment.getAmount(); // always BigDecimal
```

### When should you use each approach?

| Scenario | org.json | Jackson |
|---|---|---|
| Quick admin tool, informal data | Good enough | Overkill |
| Financial payments, strict types | Dangerous | Required |
| Attribute vs element distinction | Lost | Preserved |
| List handling (single vs array) | Ambiguous | Controlled |
| Namespace support | None | Full |

### What should a senior engineer know?

In finance, the format is secondary but the types are sacred. Routing every conversion through a typed object guarantees that an amount is always a `BigDecimal` and a currency is always a controlled value regardless of whether it arrived as JSON or XML. The conversion step and the validation step (XSD for XML, JSON Schema for JSON) are separate concerns. A senior engineer also knows that Jackson's `XmlMapper` and `ObjectMapper` share the same annotation model, so one POJO with dual annotations can serve both formats in a gateway that mediates between legacy XML partners and modern JSON clients.
